WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.860
This runnable example demonstrates a business-rule
constraint First

00:00:06.057 --> 00:00:11.625
run the completed version with a valid appointment
time PostgreSQL should accept

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:16.310
it Then change the ending time so that it is earlier
than

00:00:16.337 --> 00:00:21.665
the starting time and run the example again The SQL
syntax is

00:00:21.691 --> 00:00:25.823
still valid but the requested row now violates the
business rule enforced

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:32.015
by the CHECK constraint That difference is important
Syntax

00:00:32.042 --> 00:00:37.528
answers whether the statement is written correctly
Constraints answer whether the requested

00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:42.613
data is allowed Run both versions in the browser and
read the

00:00:42.626 --> 00:00:47.936
error message on the invalid one In pgAdmin you
would see the

00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:53.028
same idea a database-level constraint rejects bad
data regardless of which application

00:00:53.054 --> 00:00:53.725
submitted it
